{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1033 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "6c2ebed4e8d8f7b83e6eaaf9297d25bb81419ee42276375d24f9d3e39996b903" :edb/db-before-t 1032 :edb/db-after-t 1033 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534345 50 #inst "2026-10-11T19:26:26.471Z" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186200413 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419800 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186200413 1078 #edb/ref 17592186419800 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186200414 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419794 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186200414 1078 #edb/ref 17592186419794 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186200415 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419807 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186200415 1078 #edb/ref 17592186419807 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186200416 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419793 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186200416 1078 #edb/ref 17592186419793 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186200417 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419809 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186200417 1078 #edb/ref 17592186419809 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186203680 1074 "Since the modulus $n=5$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nx = 0,1,2,3,4\n$$\n\ninto the equation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot 0 & ≡0≢1 & (mod5) & \\cdot \\\\ 2 \\cdot 1 & ≡2≢1 & (mod5) & \\cdot \\\\ 2 \\cdot 2 & ≡4≢1 & (mod5) & \\cdot \\\\ 2 \\cdot 3 & ≡6≡5 + 1≡1 & (mod5) & ✓ \\\\ 2 \\cdot 4 & ≡8≡5 + 3≡3≢1 & (mod5) & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is is $x\\equiv {\\color{blue}{3}} \\:(\\text{mod}\\:5)$.\n\n**Note**: Any number congruent to $3$ modulo $5$ is a solution. For example, note that $8\\equiv 3$, and this also satisfies our congruence:\n\n$$\n2x \\mid ≡2 \\cdot 8\\begin{vmatrix}(mod5) \\\\ ≡16\\end{vmatrix}(mod5); ≡3 \\cdot 5 + 1\\begin{vmatrix}(mod5) \\\\ ≡0 + 1\\end{vmatrix}(mod5); ≡1✓ \\mid (mod5)\n$$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 false] [17592186203680 1074 "Since the modulus $n=5$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error. \n\nSubstituting $x = 0,1,2,3,4$ into the equation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 0 & ≡0≢1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times  \\\\ 2\\cdot 1 & ≡2≢1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times  \\\\ 2\\cdot 2 & ≡4≢1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times  \\\\ 2\\cdot 3 & ≡6≡5+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & ✓ \\\\ 2\\cdot 4 & ≡8≡5+3≡3≢1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is is $x\\equiv {\\color{blue}{3}} \\:(\\text{mod}\\:5).$\n\n**Note**: Any number congruent to $3$ modulo $5$ is a solution. For example, note that $8\\equiv 3,$ and this also satisfies our congruence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ≡2\\cdot 8\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡16\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 5+1\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡0+1\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡1\\,\\,✓\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186203681 1071 "Solve the modular equation $7x \\equiv 3 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:15)$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*" 13194139534345 17592186045417 false] [17592186203681 1071 "Solve the modular equation $7x \\equiv 3 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:15).$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186203681 1074 "Recall that a linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1\n$$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=7$ and $n=15$, so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(7,15)=1\n$$\n\nThis means our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$. To find the inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$, we compute the exponents of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$ until we get a result of $1 \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7^{2} & ≡49≡3 \\cdot 15 + 4≡4 & (mod15) \\\\ 7^{3} & ≡4 \\cdot 7≡28≡15 + 13≡13 & (mod15) \\\\ 7^{4} & ≡13 \\cdot 7≡91≡6 \\cdot 15 + 1≡1 & (mod15)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, $13 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$, which means that $13$ is the multiplicative inverse of $7$ (modulo $15$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $13$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7x & ≡3 & (mod15) \\\\ 13 \\cdot 7x & ≡13 \\cdot 3 & (mod15) \\\\ (13 \\cdot 7)x & ≡39 & (mod15) \\\\ 1 \\cdot x & ≡2 \\cdot 15 + 9 & (mod15) \\\\ x & ≡9 & (mod15)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\equiv 9 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:15)$." 13194139534345 17592186045417 false] [17592186203681 1074 "Recall that a linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$  \n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=7$ and $n=15,$ so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(7,15)=1.\n$$\n\nThis means our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15).$ To find the inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$, we compute the exponents of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$ until we get a result of $1 \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{2} & ≡49≡3\\cdot 15+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ {7}^{3} & ≡4\\cdot 7≡28≡15+13≡13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ {7}^{4} & ≡13\\cdot 7≡91≡6\\cdot 15+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, $13 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15),$ which means that $13$ is the multiplicative inverse of $7$ (modulo $15$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $13,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ 13\\cdot 7x & ≡13\\cdot 3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ (13\\cdot 7)x & ≡39 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡2\\cdot 15+9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x & ≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\equiv 9 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:15).$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186203682 1071 "Solve the congruence $15x \\equiv 17 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:44)$." 13194139534345 17592186045417 false] [17592186203682 1071 "Solve the congruence $15x \\equiv 17 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:44).$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186203682 1074 "Recall that a linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1\n$$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=15$ and $n=44$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(15,44)=1\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $15\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:44)$. To find the inverse of $15\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:44)$, we use the extended Euclidean algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n44 &= \\begin{vmatrix}15 \\cdot 2 & +\\end{vmatrix}14 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 15 &= \\end{vmatrix}14 \\cdot 1 \\mid + \\mid 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14 &= 44 - 15 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n1 &= 15 - 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 15 - 14 \\\\\n&= 15 - (44 - 15 \\cdot 2) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot 3 - 44\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $44$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\cdot 3 - 44 & ≡1 & (mod44) \\\\ 15 \\cdot 3 & ≡1 & (mod44)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, $15 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:44)$, which means that $3$ is the multiplicative inverse of $15$ (modulo $44$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $3$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}15x & ≡17 & (mod44) \\\\ 3 \\cdot 15x & ≡3 \\cdot 17 & (mod44) \\\\ (3 \\cdot 15)x & ≡51 & (mod44) \\\\ 1 \\cdot x & ≡44 + 7 & (mod44) \\\\ x & ≡7 & (mod44)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\equiv 7 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:44)$." 13194139534345 17592186045417 false] [17592186203682 1074 "Recall that a linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$  \n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=15$ and $n=44,$ where \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(15,44)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $15\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:44).$ To find the inverse of $15\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:44),$ we use the extended Euclidean algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}44 & = & 15\\cdot 2 & + & 14 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 15 & = & 14\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =44-15\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =15-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =15-14 \\\\  & =15-(44-15\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-44\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $44,$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\cdot 3-44 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44) \\\\ 15\\cdot 3 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $15 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:44),$ which means that $3$ is the multiplicative inverse of $15$ (modulo $44$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15x & ≡17 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44) \\\\ 3\\cdot 15x & ≡3\\cdot 17 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44) \\\\ (3\\cdot 15)x & ≡51 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡44+7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44) \\\\ x & ≡7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\equiv 7 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:44).$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186203705 1071 "Solve the following congruence:\n\n$$\n7x - 11 \\equiv 29 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:19)\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186203705 1071 "Solve the following congruence:\n$7x - 11 \\equiv 29 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:19)$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*" 13194139534345 17592186045417 false] [17592186203705 1074 "First, we reduce our congruence to a congruence of the form $ax\\equiv b$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7x - 11 & ≡29 & (mod19) \\\\ 7x - 11 + 11 & ≡29 + 11 & (mod19) \\\\ 7x & ≡40 & (mod19) \\\\ 7x & ≡38 + 2 & (mod19) \\\\ 7x & ≡2 \\cdot 19 + 2 & (mod19) \\\\ 7x & ≡2 & (mod19)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRecall that a linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1\n$$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=7$ and $n=19$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(7,19)=1\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)$. To find the inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)$, we compute the exponents of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)$ until we get a result of $1 \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7^{2} & ≡49≡2 \\cdot 19 + 11≡11 & (mod19) \\\\ 7^{3} & ≡11 \\cdot 7≡77≡4 \\cdot 19 + 1≡1 & (mod19)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, $11 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)$, which means that $11$ is the multiplicative inverse of $7$ (modulo $19$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $11$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7x & ≡2 & (mod19) \\\\ 11 \\cdot 7x & ≡11 \\cdot 2 & (mod19) \\\\ (11 \\cdot 7)x & ≡22 & (mod19) \\\\ 1 \\cdot x & ≡19 + 3 & (mod19) \\\\ x & ≡3 & (mod19)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\equiv 3 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:19)$." 13194139534345 17592186045417 false] [17592186203705 1074 "First, we reduce our congruence to a congruence of the form $ax\\equiv b,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-11 & ≡29\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 7x-11+11 & ≡29+11\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 7x & ≡40\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 7x & ≡38+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 7x & ≡2\\cdot 19+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 7x & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,19)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=7$ and $n=19$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(7,19)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19).$ To find the inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)$, we compute the exponents of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)$ until we get a result of $1 \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{2} & ≡49≡2\\cdot 19+11≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ {7}^{3} & ≡11\\cdot 7≡77≡4\\cdot 19+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $11 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:19),$ which means that $11$ is the multiplicative inverse of $7$ (modulo $19$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $11,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x & ≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 11\\cdot 7x & ≡11\\cdot 2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ (11\\cdot 7)x & ≡22 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡19+3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ x & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\equiv 3 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:19).$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186203893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419829 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186203893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419832 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186203894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419828 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186203895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419831 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186203895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419834 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186203896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419830 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186203896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419833 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186204121 1050 1020.0 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186204273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186204273 1071 "Fill in the missing values, and hence solve the modular equation $6x≡3(mod25)$. Express $x$ as a nonnegative integer smaller than $25$.$6^{2}mod25 =$\n{{field-1}} $6^{3}mod25 =$\n{{field-2}} $6^{4}mod25 =$\n{{field-3}} $6^{5}mod25 =$\n{{field-4}} $x≡$\n{{field-5}} $(mod25)$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 false] [17592186204273 1071 "Fill in the missing values, and hence solve the modular equation $6x≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25).$ Express $x$ as a nonnegative integer smaller than $25.$\n\n${6}^{2}\\,\\text{mod}\\,25=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${6}^{3}\\,\\text{mod}\\,25=$\n{{field-2}}\n\n${6}^{4}\\,\\text{mod}\\,25=$\n{{field-3}}\n\n${6}^{5}\\,\\text{mod}\\,25=$\n{{field-4}}\n\n$x≡$\n{{field-5}}$\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25)$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186204273 1074 "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=6$ and $n=25$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(6,25)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25).$ To find the inverse of $6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25)$, we compute the exponents of $6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{2} & ≡36≡25+11≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ {6}^{3} & ≡11\\cdot 6≡66≡2\\cdot 25+16≡16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ {6}^{4} & ≡16\\cdot 6≡96≡3\\cdot 25+21≡21 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ {6}^{5} & ≡21\\cdot 6≡126≡5\\cdot 25+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $21\\cdot 6≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25),$ which means that $21$ is the multiplicative inverse of $6$ (modulo $25$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $21,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ 21\\cdot 6x & ≡21\\cdot 3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ (21\\cdot 6)x & ≡63 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡2\\cdot 25+13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ x & ≡13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25).$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186205227 1064 "Suppose we want to solve the following linear congruence:\n\n$$\n4x \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)\n$$\n\nSince the modulus $n=5$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error.   \n\nSubstituting the trial solutions $x = 0,1,2,3,4$ into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot 0 & ≡0≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times  \\\\ 4\\cdot 1 & ≡4≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times  \\\\ 4\\cdot 2 & ≡8≡5+3≡3≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times  \\\\ 4\\cdot 3 & ≡12≡2\\cdot 5+2≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & ✓ \\\\ 4\\cdot 4 & ≡16≡3\\cdot 5+1≡1≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\equiv  {\\color{blue}{3}} \\:(\\text{mod}\\:5).$\n\n**Note**: Any number congruent to $3$ modulo $5$ is a solution. For example, note that $8 \\equiv 3,$ and this also satisfies our congruence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ≡4\\cdot 8\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡32\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 5+2\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡0+2\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡2\\,\\,✓\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186205227 1064 "Suppose we want to solve the following linear congruence:\n$4x \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)$\n\nSince the modulus $n=5$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error.\n\nSubstituting the trial solutions\n\n$$\nx = 0,1,2,3,4\n$$\n\ninto the equation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\cdot 0 & ≡0≢2 & (mod5) & \\cdot \\\\ 4 \\cdot 1 & ≡4≢2 & (mod5) & \\cdot \\\\ 4 \\cdot 2 & ≡8≡5 + 3≡3≢2 & (mod5) & \\cdot \\\\ 4 \\cdot 3 & ≡12≡2 \\cdot 5 + 2≡2 & (mod5) & ✓ \\\\ 4 \\cdot 4 & ≡16≡3 \\cdot 5 + 1≡1≢2 & (mod5) & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\equiv {\\color{blue}{3}} \\:(\\text{mod}\\:5)$.\n\n**Note**: Any number congruent to $3$ modulo $5$ is a solution. For example, note that $8 \\equiv 3$, and this also satisfies our congruence:\n\n$$\n4x \\mid ≡4 \\cdot 8\\begin{vmatrix}(mod5) \\\\ ≡32\\end{vmatrix}(mod5); ≡6 \\cdot 5 + 2\\begin{vmatrix}(mod5) \\\\ ≡0 + 2\\end{vmatrix}(mod5); ≡2✓ \\mid (mod5)\n$$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 false] [17592186205228 1064 "Now, let's consider the following linear congruence:\n\n$$\n4x \\equiv 5 \\qquad (\\text{mod}\\:21)\n$$\n\nWe can solve this congruence by multiplying both sides by the *multiplicative inverse* of $4$ modulo $21$.\n\nIn this case, we can find the multiplicative inverse by raising $4$ to positive integer exponents until we get an answer of $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4^{2} & ≡16 & (mod21) \\\\ 4^{3} & ≡4 \\cdot 16≡64≡3 \\cdot 21 + 1≡3 \\cdot 0 + 1≡1 & (mod21)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, $4 \\cdot {\\color{blue}{16}} \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:21)$, which means that $16$ is the multiplicative inverse of $4$ (modulo $21$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $16$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4x & ≡5 & (mod21) \\\\ 16 \\cdot 4x & ≡16 \\cdot 5 & (mod21) \\\\ (16 \\cdot 4)x & ≡80 & (mod21) \\\\ 1 \\cdot x & ≡3 \\cdot 21 + 17 & (mod21) \\\\ x & ≡17 & (mod21)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\equiv 17 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:21)$." 13194139534345 17592186045417 false] [17592186205228 1064 "Now, let's consider the following linear congruence:\n\n$$\n4x \\equiv 5 \\qquad (\\text{mod}\\:21)\n$$\n\nWe can solve this congruence by multiplying both sides by the *multiplicative inverse* of $4$ modulo $21.$ \n\nIn this case, we can find the multiplicative inverse by raising $4$ to positive integer exponents until we get an answer of $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2} & ≡16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ {4}^{3} & ≡4\\cdot 16≡64≡3\\cdot 21+1≡3\\cdot 0+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $4 \\cdot {\\color{blue}{16}} \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:21),$ which means that $16$ is the multiplicative inverse of $4$ (modulo $21$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $16,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ 16\\cdot 4x & ≡16\\cdot 5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ (16\\cdot 4)x & ≡80 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡3\\cdot 21+17 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ x & ≡17 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\equiv 17 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:21).$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186205229 1064 "Not every linear congruence has a unique solution!\n\nTo determine whether a linear congruence has a single unique solution, we can use the following rule:\n\n> *A linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1$.*\n\nWe'll prove this theorem at the end of the lesson.\n\nSo, whenever we solve a linear congruence, we should first use the above rule to check that there is a unique solution. In the linear congruence $4x \\equiv 5 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:21)$, we had $a=4$ and $n=21$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(4,21)=1\n$$\n\nwhich means that our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nIn a future lesson, we'll discuss cases where linear congruences have multiple or no solutions." 13194139534345 17592186045417 false] [17592186205229 1064 "Not every linear congruence has a unique solution! \n\nTo determine whether a linear congruence has a single unique solution, we can use the following rule:\n\n*A linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$ *\n\nWe'll prove this theorem at the end of the lesson.\n\nSo, whenever we solve a linear congruence, we should first use the above rule to check that there is a unique solution. In the linear congruence $4x \\equiv 5 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:21),$ we had $a=4$ and $n=21.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(4,21)=1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that our congruence does indeed have a unique solution. \n\nIn a future lesson, we'll discuss cases where linear congruences have multiple or no solutions." 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186205230 1064 "Let's prove the following theorem:\n\n*Let $a$, $b,$ and $n$ integers. If $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1$, then the linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$  has a unique solution.*\n\n**Proof**.  \n\nTo prove this theorem, we must show that a solution exists and that it is unique. \n\nFirst, we prove that a solution exists:\n\n- If $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1$, then by Bézout's identity, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that \n\n$$\nau+nv=1.\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}au+nv & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,n) \\\\ au & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis means that $u$ is the multiplicative inverse of $a$ (modulo $n$).\n\nSo, multiplying both sides of the original congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ by $u,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,n) \\\\ u\\cdot ax & ≡u\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,n) \\\\ (u\\cdot a)x & ≡ub &  & (\\text{mod}\\,n) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡ub &  & (\\text{mod}\\,n) \\\\ x & ≡ub &  & (\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x \\equiv ub \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n)$ is a solution.\n\nNext, we prove the solution is unique:\n\n- To prove uniqueness, we show that two solutions $x_1$ and $x_2$ to our congruence must be congruent. \n\nSuppose $x_1$ and $x_2$ are two solutions to the congruence, i.e., $ax_1 \\equiv b\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$ and $ax_2 \\equiv b\\:  (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$. We want to show that $x_1 \\equiv x_2$.\n\nSince $ax_1 \\equiv b\\:  (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$ and $ax_2 \\equiv b\\:  (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$, we have\n\n$$\nax_1 \\equiv ax_2  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n}).\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\text{gcd}(a,n) = 1$, we can multiply both sides by the modular inverse of $a$ modulo $n ,$ which we previously denoted $u.$ \n\n$$\n(u \\cdot a) x_1 \\equiv (u \\cdot a) x_2  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})\n$$\n\nSince $a$ and $n$ are relatively prime, $u$ exists. Therefore, $u \\cdot a \\equiv 1\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$, and we have\n\n$$\nx_1 \\equiv x_2  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n}),\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nThus, we have shown both the existence and uniqueness of the solution to the congruence $ax \\equiv b \\pmod{n}$ when $\\text{gcd}(a,n) = 1$." 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186205230 1064 "Let's prove the following theorem:\n\n> *Let $a$, $b$, and $n$ integers. If $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1$, then the linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution.*\n\n**Proof**.\n\nTo prove this theorem, we must show that a solution exists and that it is unique.\n\nFirst, we prove that a solution exists:\n\n- If $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1$, then by Bézout's identity, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n$au+nv=1$.\nWe can write this result in modulo $n$ as follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}au + nv & ≡1 & (modn) \\\\ au & ≡1 & (modn)\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis means that $u$ is the multiplicative inverse of $a$ (modulo $n$).So, multiplying both sides of the original congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ by $u$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}ax & ≡b & (modn) \\\\ u \\cdot ax & ≡u \\cdot b & (modn) \\\\ (u \\cdot a)x & ≡ub & (modn) \\\\ 1 \\cdot x & ≡ub & (modn) \\\\ x & ≡ub & (modn)\\end{bmatrix}$\nTherefore, $x \\equiv ub \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n)$ is a solution.\n\nNext, we prove the solution is unique:\n\n- To prove uniqueness, we show that two solutions $x_1$ and $x_2$ to our congruence must be congruent. Suppose $x_1$ and $x_2$ are two solutions to the congruence, i.e., $ax_1 \\equiv b\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$ and $ax_2 \\equiv b\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$. We want to show that $x_1 \\equiv x_2$.Since $ax_1 \\equiv b\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$ and $ax_2 \\equiv b\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$, we have\n$ax_1 \\equiv ax_2 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$.Now, since $\\text{gcd}(a,n) = 1$, we can multiply both sides by the modular inverse of $a$ modulo $n$, which we previously denoted $u$.\n$(u \\cdot a) x_1 \\equiv (u \\cdot a) x_2 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$Since $a$ and $n$ are relatively prime, $u$ exists. Therefore, $u \\cdot a \\equiv 1\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$, and we have\n$x_1 \\equiv x_2 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$,\nas required.\n\nThus, we have shown both the existence and uniqueness of the solution to the congruence $ax \\equiv b \\pmod{n}$ when\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n) = 1\n$$\n\n." 13194139534345 17592186045417 false] [17592186419793 1080 "13" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419793 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419793 1083 #uuid "088c8755-232f-5d67-b714-cb5f32d8ba5e" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419794 1080 "1" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419794 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419794 1083 #uuid "23ebcbf3-35d1-5715-bd12-20face8731a4" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419795 1080 "4" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419795 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419795 1083 #uuid "3bbe80cc-77d0-5bec-b9a3-584c08cefb71" 13194139534345 17592186045417 tr