&  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {4}^{4} & ≡9\\cdot 4≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {4}^{5} & ≡3\\cdot 4≡12≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $3\\cdot 4≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11),$ which means that $3$ is the multiplicative inverse of $4$ (modulo $11$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ≡7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ 3\\cdot 4x & ≡3\\cdot 7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ (3\\cdot 4)x & ≡21 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡1\\cdot 11+10 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ x & ≡10 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡10\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419822 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419829 1069 "q-214736" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419829 1071 "Solve the linear congruence $5x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$ using trial and error. Express your answer as a nonnegative integer smaller than $6.$\n\n$x≡$\n{{field-1}}$\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419829 1072 #uuid "2c11ba83-6848-5a89-900b-5fb2ae872fa8" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419829 1074 "Since the modulus $n=6$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error. \n\nSubstituting $x=0,1,2,3,4,5$ into the congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot 0 & ≡0≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) &  & \\times  \\\\ 5\\cdot 1 & ≡5≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) &  & \\times  \\\\ 5\\cdot 2 & ≡10≡6+4≡4≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) &  & \\times  \\\\ 5\\cdot 3 & ≡15≡2\\cdot 6+3≡3≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) &  & \\times  \\\\ 5\\cdot 4 & ≡20≡3\\cdot 6+2≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) &  & ✓ \\\\ 5\\cdot 5 & ≡25≡4\\cdot 6+1≡1≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) &  & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).$\n\n**Note**: Any number congruent to $4$ modulo $6$ is a solution. For example, note that $10≡4,$ and this also satisfies our congruence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & ≡5\\cdot 10\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡50\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 6+2\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡0+2\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\,\\,✓\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419824 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419830 1069 "q-132295" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419830 1071 "Solve the congruence $35x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51).$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419830 1072 #uuid "3b5eee82-5740-5d92-bedf-cc94230c0e60" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419830 1074 "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=35$ and $n=51$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(35,51)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $35\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51).$ To find the inverse of $35\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51)$, use the extended Euclidean algorithm.  \n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}51 & = & 35\\cdot 1 & + & 16 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 35 & = & 16\\cdot 2 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 16 & = & 3\\cdot 5 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =51-35\\cdot 1 \\\\ 3 & =35-16\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =16-3\\cdot 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =16-3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =16-(35-16\\cdot 2)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =16\\cdot 11-35\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =(51-35)\\cdot 11-35\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =51\\cdot 11-35\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =51\\cdot 11+35\\cdot (-16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n1=51\\cdot 11+35\\cdot (-16).\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $51,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}51\\cdot 11+35\\cdot (-16) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ 35\\cdot (-16) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ 35\\cdot (51-16) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ 35\\cdot 35 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $35\\cdot 35≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51),$ which means that \n$35$  is its own multiplicative inverse ( (modulo $51$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $35,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}35x & ≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ 35\\cdot 35x & ≡35\\cdot 5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ (35\\cdot 35)x & ≡175 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡3\\cdot 51+22 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ x & ≡22 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡22\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51).$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419825 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419831 1069 "q-213507" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419831 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419831 1071 "Solve the following congruence:\n\n$$\n5x+4≡23\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419831 1072 #uuid "3db815a9-d784-52dd-bf7e-2dc644a57255" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419831 1074 "First, we reduce our congruence to a congruence of the form $ax≡b,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+4 & ≡23\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 5x+4-4 & ≡23-4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 5x & ≡19\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 5x & ≡1\\cdot 13+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 5x & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=5$ and $n=13$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(5,13)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$ To find the inverse of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$, we compute the exponents of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2} & ≡25≡1\\cdot 13+12≡12 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {5}^{3} & ≡12\\cdot 5≡60≡4\\cdot 13+8≡8 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {5}^{4} & ≡8\\cdot 5≡40≡3\\cdot 13+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $8\\cdot 5≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13),$ which means that $8$ is the multiplicative inverse of $5$ (modulo $13$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $8,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & ≡6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 8\\cdot 5x & ≡8\\cdot 6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ (8\\cdot 5)x & ≡48 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡9+3\\cdot 13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ x & ≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419823 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419832 1069 "q-214738" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419832 1071 "Solve the linear congruence $3x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$ using trial and error. Express your answer as a nonnegative integer smaller than $7.$\n\n$x≡$\n{{field-1}}$\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419832 1072 #uuid "4f60f67b-c6e2-55b6-a159-dee4ac2e0060" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419832 1074 "Since the modulus $n=7$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error. \n\nSubstituting $x=0,1,2,3,4,5,6$ into the congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 0 & ≡0≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 1 & ≡3≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 2 & ≡6≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 3 & ≡9≡7+2≡2≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 4 & ≡12≡7+5≡5≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 5 & ≡15≡2\\cdot 7+1≡1≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 6 & ≡18≡2\\cdot 7+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).$\n\n**Note**: Any number congruent to $6$ modulo $7$ is a solution. For example, note that $13≡6,$ and this also satisfies our congruence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ≡3\\cdot 13\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡39\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡5\\cdot 7+4\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡0+4\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4\\,\\,✓\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419833 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419827 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419833 1069 "q-78069" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419833 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419833 1071 "Solve the congruence $15x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43).$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419833 1072 #uuid "6fe75766-c260-5668-9726-1bdf3c8f2f17" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419833 1074 "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=15$ and $n=43$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(15,43)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43).$ To find the inverse of $15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43)$, use the extended Euclidean algorithm. \n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}43 & = & 15\\cdot 2 & + & 13 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 15 & = & 13\\cdot 1 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 13 & = & 2\\cdot 6 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =43-15\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2 & =15-13 \\\\ 1 & =13-2\\cdot 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =13-2\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =13-(15-13)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =13\\cdot 7-15\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =(43-15\\cdot 2)\\cdot 7-15\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =43\\cdot 7-15\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =43\\cdot 7+15\\cdot (-20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n1=43\\cdot 7+15\\cdot (-20).\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $43,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}43\\cdot 7+15\\cdot (-20) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 15\\cdot (-20) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 15\\cdot (43-20) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 15\\cdot 23 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $15\\cdot 23≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43),$ which means that $23$ is the multiplicative inverse of $15$ (modulo $43$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $23,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15x & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 23\\cdot 15x & ≡23\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ (23\\cdot 15)x & ≡92 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡92 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ x & ≡6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43).$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419821 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419834 1069 "q-213522" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419834 1071 "Solve the following congruence:\n\n$$\n4x+3≡34\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13),\n$$\n\nexpressing your answer as a nonnegative integer smaller than $13.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*\n\n$x≡$\n{{field-1}}$\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419834 1072 #uuid "7b2d85f9-70d5-5d8a-aa2a-9b3b30df44ef" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true] [17592186419834 1074 "First, we reduce our congruence to a congruence of the form $ax≡b,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3 & ≡34\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 4x+3-3 & ≡34-3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 4x & ≡31\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 4x & ≡2\\cdot 13+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 4x & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=4$ and $n=13$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(4,13)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$ To find the inverse of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$, we compute the exponents of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2} & ≡16≡1\\cdot 13+3≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {4}^{3} & ≡3\\cdot 4≡12 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {4}^{4} & ≡12\\cdot 4≡48≡3\\cdot 13+9≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {4}^{5} & ≡9\\cdot 4≡36≡2\\cdot 13+10≡10 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {4}^{6} & ≡10\\cdot 4≡40≡3\\cdot 13+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $10\\cdot 4≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13),$ which means that $10$ is the multiplicative inverse of $4$ (modulo $13$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $10,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 10\\cdot 4x & ≡10\\cdot 5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ (10\\cdot 4)x & ≡50 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡11+3\\cdot 13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ x & ≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡11\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$" 13194139534345 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-088c8755-232f-5d67-b714-cb5f32d8ba5e" 17592186419793 "answer-23ebcbf3-35d1-5715-bd12-20face8731a4" 17592186419794 "answer-3bbe80cc-77d0-5bec-b9a3-584c08cefb71" 17592186419795 "answer-3d34f638-9a0e-5ca4-a67d-6e32e086b9f1" 17592186419796 "answer-424edbb9-4ced-5f21-bbeb-ac5e675871e7" 17592186419797 "answer-44378403-b1a6-51ab-bdb4-8a83dc56f909" 17592186419798 "answer-44efd338-666a-5ada-a785-765da28084d0" 17592186419799 "answer-45591275-4f92-5c6a-95be-4ec70c990168" 17592186419800 "answer-67b9f08f-ccc6-5559-9c89-4b109a747d41" 17592186419801 "answer-680844b9-8e08-5d3b-87b4-0ea3de54ee20" 17592186419802 "answer-69e7f375-a389-5bdc-89d6-4d38cccfbe8c" 17592186419803 "answer-7ddb8243-ef75-5cc7-9d5f-07daa908cd86" 17592186419804 "answer-882df7c4-3a86-5d50-8e6d-f1ec40ab280a" 17592186419805 "answer-925492cf-b212-5f24-a956-5e4ebd50534c" 17592186419806 "answer-a41993f1-642c-526e-83fb-2308acfe075f" 17592186419807 "answer-a57931da-2515-5c40-8190-f20e4d544a5e" 17592186419808 "answer-ad76e3b5-29b9-5bea-a8d9-e0706604d1bc" 17592186419809 "answer-b19af155-55c5-534a-b966-b4cb8b712651" 17592186419810 "answer-b95b1927-2dd5-58b1-bd34-01d6ec3c0908" 17592186419811 "answer-c0555944-5da1-5f24-9981-3b0e3a814a45" 17592186419812 "answer-d256f5a7-e3af-5e6b-8f30-286d8ceacb40" 17592186419813 "answer-e2f78442-1c48-529e-80f1-950ea1923966" 17592186419814 "answer-e5ea6e5b-5c39-5cf4-a983-f88e1861ecd4" 17592186419815 "answer-e5fd8304-33a3-582c-8a4f-dbb98a08be96" 17592186419816 "answer-f4efd977-d8da-5378-8891-6af2bd5f45d7" 17592186419817 "answer-f6e32835-b8b0-5ef3-9fcc-e5ddf1c98c10" 17592186419818 "answer-f740ef79-86a1-5132-98b5-754715a1cf95" 17592186419819 "answer-fb1c70ff-3515-5c02-9286-ee2663506546" 17592186419820 "answer-field-05b43649-64c4-5c3e-ae9c-72a41cf3ebb6" 17592186419821 "answer-field-0d2633ee-1d5b-5783-bb62-c0caed17ffa5" 17592186419822 "answer-field-2f9f0ad8-9183-59f0-8835-5839bbca3f42" 1